{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>At that time HaShem said to me: hew for yourself</b>. After I prostrated myself before HaShem for the forty days and the forty nights, He was appeased toward me to write second tablets, but the first ones had been the work of Elohim and the writing was the writing of Elohim, whereas in these He commanded me that they be hewn by my hand, and the writing would be like the first writing, by the finger of Elohim." "The sense of <b>and you shall make for yourself an ark of wood</b> is that you shall place the tablets inside it at the time you descend, and this ark was entirely of wood — it and the cover upon it from above, as is the manner of all arks. And the tablets remained there until the Tabernacle was made, and then they made the ark overlaid with gold and the cover upon it of pure gold. And He did not say thus concerning the first tablets, because it was disclosed before Him that he would break them.\n\nThe sense of <i>and they were there</i> (Deuteronomy 10:5), <i>as HaShem commanded me</i> — they were there until the Tabernacle was made, concerning which He commanded me (Exodus 25:21), <i>and you shall place the cover upon the ark from above, and into the ark you shall put the testimony that I shall give you</i>. And this is the good and correct reading in these verses.\n\nRashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 10:1): this is not the ark that Bezalel made, for they were not engaged in the Tabernacle except after the Day of Atonement, for when he descended from the mountain he commanded them concerning the work of the Tabernacle, and Bezalel made the Tabernacle first and afterward the ark and the vessels; it follows that this was another ark. And this is the one that went out with them to war, whereas the one Bezalel made did not go out to war except in the days of Eli, and they were punished on its account and it was captured. This is the language of the Rav, and they are words of Aggadah that he found written in Tanḥuma.\n\nOne may ask: after he removed the tablets from there and placed them in the ark Bezalel made, what remained in this one, and why did it go out with them to war? And they say that the broken fragments of the tablets were there, and so it is in the Aggadah. But this is the view of an individual, for thus we learned in tractate Shekalim (Jerusalem Talmudi 6:1): it was taught, Rabbi Yehudah bar Rabbi Ilai says, two arks went with Israel in the wilderness — one in which the broken fragments of the tablets were placed, etc. — but the Rabbis say there was only one, and it went out once in the days of Eli and was captured.\n\nThe view of our Sages everywhere in the Talmud (Berakhot 8b; Bava Batra 14a; Menahot 99a) is not so, but rather the tablets and the broken fragments of the tablets were placed in the ark. And furthermore, where would this ark stand together with the first tablets all the days of the wilderness? For in the Tabernacle, in the Holy of Holies behind the curtain, there are not two arks there, and so too Solomon brought only one ark into the Holy of Holies (I Kings 8:6–9).\n\nBut this ark of Moses — when the ark of Bezalel was made, they stored it away according to the law of sacred utensils. And this is the correct view according to the opinion of our Sages. For according to the plain sense, it is possible that <i>and you shall make for yourself an ark of wood</i> alludes to the ark Bezalel made, for at first Moses was commanded concerning the Tabernacle and its vessels, and the first commandment was <i>and they shall make an ark of acacia wood</i> (Exodus 25:10), for it is the principal purpose of the entire Tabernacle — that HaShem sit upon the cherubim. And afterward they made the calf, and when HaShem was appeased toward Moses and told him to write on these tablets like the first writing, He commanded him in brief to make an ark of wood for these tablets — this being what was commanded for the first tablets. And here He reminded him of the first commandment concerning the matter of the Tabernacle, that everything depends upon it, and from this Moses understood to make the Tabernacle and its vessels as he had been commanded at first.\n\nThe meaning of <i>and they were there as HaShem commanded me</i> would then be that they were there forever, as HaShem had commanded him at first (Exodus 25:21–22), <i>and into the ark you shall put the testimony that I shall give you, and I will meet with you there and I will speak with you</i>, etc. Now when he descended, he set the tablets in the Tent of Meeting until the ark and the Tabernacle would be made, for had he not broken the first tablets, they would have remained in the Tent of Meeting until Bezalel made the ark; and the broken fragments of the tablets were perforce there as well, for he did not bring them back with him to the mountain.\n\nProof for this is that Moses did not mention this command in the portion of Ki Tissa, for there Bezalel's ark is mentioned explicitly, whereas here he did not mention the Tabernacle and its vessels because they see it, and therefore he mentioned this in brief.\n\nLet it not be difficult in your eyes that he said <i>and I made an ark of acacia wood</i> and <i>and I hewed two tablets of stone</i>, for he treated the matter of the ark as one. For HaShem commanded him <i>hew for yourself</i>, etc., <i>and go up to Me to the mountain</i>, and <i>you shall make for yourself an ark of wood</i> — that he should go up first with the tablets and afterward make the ark of wood. But he went on at length to say <i>and I wrote on the tablets the words that were on the first tablets which you broke, and I placed them in the ark</i>, to say that they would be like the first ones in their writing, and that it is fitting that they be in the commanded ark like the first ones, for <i>I will meet there with the children of Israel and it shall be sanctified through My glory</i>. And therefore he completed <i>and I made an ark</i> to complete the commandment and the deed in brief, and afterward returned to elaborate on what preceded.\n\nn: The page references inserted parenthetically within the Hebrew text of comment 1 (e.g., 'ברכות ח ב\"ב יד מנחות צט') appear to be a later editor's glosses identifying the Talmudic passages; I have rendered them as standardized daf references."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:48:27.642839876Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}